        <div id="what-500-buys" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What $500 Buys — and What It Does Not</h2>
<p>
            A Black Friday discount does not change what a machine is. It is worth being clear
            about the limits of this tier before the sale starts, because the biggest percentages of
            the week will be on machines that are being asked to do more than they can.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mostly, it buys walking.</strong> Under $500 new, the realistic options are
            walking pads, 2-in-1 pads with a fold-up bar, compact folding machines with short decks,
            and manual walkers. Every pad on this list tops out at around 4 mph with the bar down,
            and that is not a setting you can change: the belt, the motor and the frame are sized
            for walking. If walking is what you will do — at a desk, in the evening, on a slope —
            this tier is genuinely good value, and a sale makes it better.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It does not buy a running treadmill.</strong> Running needs a long deck, a
            motor with continuous power to spare and a frame heavy enough not to move. Most runners
            want a 60-inch deck; the one full-size machine here has a 55-inch belt, which is fine for
            walking and light jogging and short for a running stride at speed, and retailers list
            its belt as 18 inches wide where 20 is more usual. Walking pads have belts of 40 to 48
            inches. A 2-in-1 that reaches 6.2 mph with its bar raised can carry a short, light jog;
            it is not a machine to train on. If running is the point, the useful Black Friday
            question is how far below $1,000 a 60-inch machine falls, which is what our
            <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-under-1000/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday deals under $1,000</a>
            page covers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It rarely buys margin for heavier users.</strong> Capacities on this list run
            from 242 lb to 400 lb, but a weight rating is a ceiling, not a comfortable operating
            point, and on cheap pads the motor's continuous rating is usually well under 1 HP. The
            one exception here is the MERACH W50 TrekPad, which publishes a continuous motor rating
            of 1.25 HP alongside its 400 lb capacity. If you are close to a machine's rating, buy
            the next one up rather than the cheapest one that technically fits.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor figures are the least reliable number on the box.</strong> "2.5 HP" or
            "3.5 HP" on a walking pad is a peak figure. Independent trackers that read the manuals
            and motor labels typically derive something like 0.75 to 1 HP of continuous output from
            the same pads. That is enough for walking, which is what they are for, but it is why a
            heavy discount on a "3.5 HP running treadmill" from an unfamiliar brand should make you
            more cautious, not less.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this means you should spend more. It means matching the machine to the use and
            then buying it at the best price you can. Our
            <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best budget treadmills</a>
            guide sets out one pick per price band if you are still deciding where your budget
            should sit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="discounts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Big Percentages, Small Savings: How Discounts Work in This Tier</h2>
<p>
            Budget folding treadmills and walking pads attract the largest headline percentages of
            the year and the most inflated reference prices. The prices we saw on 28 October show
            why, a month before the sale.
          </p>
          <p>
            UREVO's store had the Strol 2E at $169.99 against a "compare at" price of $289.99,
            which reads as 41% off; the same $169.99 was on the same page on 13 October, so it is
            the normal selling price, not a promotion. The SpaceWalk 3S was $249.99 against
            $349.99, also unchanged since mid-October. MERACH showed the W50 TrekPad at $299.99
            against $479.99 on 18 October, and an independent tracker had seen it at $296.99 on
            Amazon on 1 September. WalkingPad's Z1 was $299 against $329. None of those reference
            prices describe what anyone has recently paid. Measured against them, a Black Friday
            price of $160 on the Strol 2E would be "45% off"; measured against what it actually
            sells for, it is about 6%.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is the arithmetic to do with every deal in this tier: compare the sale price with
            the price the machine sold for in the weeks before, not with the crossed-out number. A
            genuine treadmill reduction in a strong sale window is commonly somewhere around 15 to
            30% off the normal selling price, as our
            <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/#which-tiers" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">method page explains</a>.
            On a $170 pad, 15% is about $25. Real, but not worth rushing an unsuitable machine for.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three other things shape prices here. <strong>Coupons and promo codes</strong> are
            common on walking pad listings — a clickable coupon under the price or a seller code
            posted on deal sites — and they can produce the genuinely lowest prices of the year: the
            lowest W50 TrekPad price we found was $197.99 with an Amazon promo code in July 2026,
            well below its usual price. <strong>Cart-only prices</strong> appear on some full-size
            listings, including NordicTrack's, where the price is shown only once the item is in
            your cart. And <strong>prices move between Amazon and the brand's own store</strong>,
            sometimes by $20 to $50 in either direction, so check both before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. When the 2026 Deals Start</h2>
<p>
            Black Friday is Friday 27 November 2026 and Cyber Monday is Monday 30 November. Amazon
            had not announced its 2026 Black Friday dates when we checked on 28 October; it rarely
            confirms them far in advance. Based on previous years, this is the typical shape:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Now to mid-November.</strong> Brand stores run their own sales already —
            UREVO's site was running a Halloween promotion on 28 October. Treat this period as your
            baseline: write down what each machine on your shortlist sells for now, on Amazon and
            on the brand's store, with the date. That figure is what you will measure Black Friday
            against.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The week before Thanksgiving.</strong> Amazon's Black Friday event typically
            begins in the week or so before Thanksgiving, which falls on 26 November this year, and
            many walking pad brands time their Amazon coupons to match. Prices on
            budget pads often reach their sale level at the start of the event rather than on the
            Friday itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Black Friday to Cyber Monday (27–30 November).</strong> Some deals change,
            some are extended and a few improve. Limited-time and limited-quantity deals on Amazon
            can sell out in hours; if a machine already on your shortlist hits your target price,
            there is little to gain from waiting for Cyber Monday. Our
            <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday 2026 treadmill deals hub</a>
            follows the whole market across those four days.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Early December.</strong> Prices on pads frequently settle back to their
            October level, and the January fitness rush follows. If you miss the sale and the
            price on your shortlist is no worse than your October baseline, you have lost very
            little.
          </p>
          <p>
            One practical note: holiday return windows at large retailers are often extended for
            purchases made in November and December, but the terms vary by retailer, seller and
            item, and a marketplace seller's own policy may apply instead. Read the return terms on
            the listing, not a general announcement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What to Check Before You Buy at This Price</h2>
<p>
            At $500 and below, the listing is often a bigger risk than the machine. These checks
            take ten minutes and catch most of the problems we came across while putting this list
            together.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The exact model.</strong> Cheap machines come in families with similar names
            and different specifications. The Strol 2E is flat; the Strol 2E Pro has incline. The
            W50 TrekPad is rated to 400 lb; the W50 Lite is a different pad with a lower rating and
            a 10% incline. Sunny's SF-T1407SMART has Bluetooth; the older SF-T1407M does not. Black
            Friday deal pages often link to whichever variant is cheapest, so match the model
            number on the order page to the one you researched.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The seller.</strong> On Amazon, read the "sold by" line near the buy button.
            Amazon itself or the brand's own storefront is what you want; an unrelated reseller may
            be selling old stock or a machine the brand will not warrant. A deal that only exists
            from a third-party seller with a different name from the brand deserves a second look.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The weight rating and belt length.</strong> Take the lower figure when a
            listing contradicts itself, as several in this tier do. For walking, a belt of 40 inches
            or more suits most adults; taller walkers are more comfortable on 45 inches or more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The warranty and how to activate it.</strong> Several pad brands give one year
            as standard and add months only if you register the machine in their app. Do it on the
            day it arrives, and keep the order confirmation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How it arrives.</strong> Walking pads come as parcels of roughly 40 to 70 lb,
            left at the door. A full-size treadmill like the T Series 5 comes as freight and has to
            be assembled. Check what delivery level the listing includes before you order, not when
            the truck arrives.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cheap machines also reward a little care once they are home. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-make-a-cheap-treadmill-last-longer/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making a cheap treadmill last longer</a>
            covers belt tension, lubrication and the habits that decide whether a $200 pad lasts
            one year or several.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="traps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Traps Specific to Cheap Black Friday Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            Most Black Friday traps apply to every treadmill. A few are particular to this tier,
            because it is where marketplace brands compete hardest and where buyers have the least
            room for a mistake.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The unfamiliar brand at 60% off.</strong> Budget walking pads are sold under a
            constantly changing set of brand names. Some are perfectly decent; some have no working
            support line once the sale is over. Before buying a brand you have not heard of, look
            for a website outside Amazon, a published warranty and a way to reach the company. And
            check the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's site: in April 2026 the CPSC told
            owners of Sperax walking pads to stop using them after reports of uncontrolled speed,
            falls and fires, and the importer declined a recall. That is why no Sperax pad appears
            here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>"Running treadmill" in the title.</strong> Listing titles at this price use
            "running" generously. A 40-inch belt and a peak-rated motor do not make a running
            machine, whatever the title says or however fast the display goes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Renewed and open-box listings in the deal feed.</strong> They can be good value
            and often sit right next to new units in Black Friday results, at a lower price. Check
            the condition line and the warranty: a renewed pad may carry a shorter warranty than a
            new one, and the saving is not always large enough to justify that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bundles priced as savings.</strong> A pad "bundled" with a mat, a remote stand
            or a desk tray can be good value or simply a way to make a price comparison impossible.
            Price the machine on its own on the same day; if the bundle costs more than the machine
            plus a mat bought separately, it is not a deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget-moves" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. If Your Budget Moves Up or Down</h2>
<p>
            The under-$500 tier sits between two quite different markets, and a Black Friday sale
            can move a machine from one into the other.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have closer to $300,</strong> this list already covers most of it —
            four of the six usually sell for $300 or less. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $300</a>
            guide adds the cheapest pads worth considering, including a flat pad that sells for
            under $170. At that price you are buying a walking pad, and that is fine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you can stretch past $500,</strong> the value of each extra dollar is
            higher than at any other point in the market. A 60-inch deck, a heavier frame and a
            longer warranty appear somewhere between $700 and $1,000, and Black Friday is when
            those machines come closest to this tier. If you intend to run, it is worth comparing
            the best sale price on a 60-inch machine with the best on the T Series 5 before
            deciding.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mainly want a pad for a desk,</strong> our sibling page on
            <a href="/black-friday-walking-pad-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday walking pad deals</a>
            covers more pads in more detail, including quieter and higher-capacity options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you get a good treadmill for under $500 on Black Friday?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you want to walk. Walking pads, 2-in-1 pads and manual walkers all sell for well under $500, and a sale makes them better value. A full-size treadmill is harder: the NordicTrack T Series 5 usually sells for about $590 to $600 and has been recorded as low as $418.99 on Amazon, so it can dip under $500 in a sale. A machine built for regular running usually costs more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good Black Friday price for a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Judge it against what the pad sold for in October, not the crossed-out list price. Many pads already sell 30 to 40% below their list prices every day. A genuine Black Friday reduction is usually somewhere around 15 to 30% off the normal selling price; on a $250 pad that is roughly $40 to $75.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When do Amazon Black Friday treadmill deals start in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Amazon had not announced its 2026 dates when we checked on 28 October. Its Black Friday event typically starts in the week before Thanksgiving, which falls on 26 November this year, and runs through Cyber Monday on 30 November. Budget pads often reach their sale price at the start of the event rather than on the Friday itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run on a treadmill that costs under $500?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only lightly. Walking pads top out around 4 mph, and 2-in-1 pads reach about 6 mph on a belt of around 40 inches. The NordicTrack T Series 5 reaches 10 mph, but its 55-inch belt is short for running at speed. If you plan to run regularly, a 60-inch deck is the better target, which usually means spending more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cheap treadmill with a 3.5 HP motor good for running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on that figure alone. On budget pads, the advertised horsepower is a peak figure, and independent trackers that read the manuals typically derive under 1 HP of continuous output. The continuous rating is the one that matters for sustained use, and few cheap machines publish it; the MERACH W50 TrekPad, at 1.25 HP continuous, is an exception.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a cheap treadmill now or wait for Black Friday?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you need it now and the price matches what it has sold for recently, the saving from waiting is likely to be modest on a pad that already costs $170 to $300. If you can wait, record today's price on Amazon and the brand's own store, then buy when it falls clearly below that figure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Under-$500 Black Friday Deals</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come down to judging a machine by its discount instead of by what it will do.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the discount against the list price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Budget pads routinely sell 30 to 40% below their printed list prices on ordinary days. A Black Friday banner quoting the list price can look like a large saving when the real reduction is a few dollars. Record the price each machine on your shortlist sells for in October and measure against that.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a walking pad to run on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 40-inch belt and a peak-rated motor are built for walking. A pad that reaches 6 mph with its bar raised can carry a short, light jog, not regular running. If running is the goal, compare the best sale price on a 60-inch machine before settling on a cheaper one that will not do the job.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying close to the weight limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A weight rating is a ceiling, not a comfortable operating point, and cheap pads have little motor power in reserve. If you are within about 30 lb of a machine’s rating, choose one rated higher, such as a 400 lb pad, rather than the cheapest one that technically fits.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking the model number on the deal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Budget machines come in families with near-identical names and different specifications — Strol 2E and Strol 2E Pro, W50 TrekPad and W50 Lite. Deal pages often link to whichever variant is cheapest. Match the model number on the order page to the one you researched before you pay.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

